zink: disk cache ID from the binary's timestamp without ELF build-id

Without an ELF build-id, zink's disk cache ID now hashes the
modification time of the binary that contains zink (dladdr + stat, as
disk_cache_get_function_identifier does); without that timestamp zink
runs without a disk cache. On Apple platforms the ID was only MoltenVK's
pipelineCacheUUID plus zink's flags, and the cache key has no Mesa
version: after an app update (Library/Caches survives it) a build with
changed Mesa code could load NIR finalized by the previous build. Mesa
is linked statically into the app, whose executable is relinked by
every build. ELF builds are unchanged.

Upstream: not submitted; Mesa 26 uses the Mach-O LC_UUID instead
(util/build_id.c), drop this patch on an upgrade.

--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
@@ -316,6 +316,15 @@
    unsigned build_id_len = build_id_length(note);
    assert(note && build_id_len == 20); /* blake3 */
    _mesa_blake3_update(&ctx, build_id_data(note), build_id_len);
+#elif defined(HAVE_DLADDR)
+   /* No ELF build-id (e.g. Mach-O): hash in the timestamp of the binary containing zink instead, as
+    * disk_cache_get_function_identifier does, so another build never reads this build's NIR. No timestamp
+    * -> no disk cache.
+    */
+   uint32_t timestamp;
+   if (!disk_cache_get_function_timestamp(disk_cache_init, &timestamp))
+      return true;
+   _mesa_blake3_update(&ctx, &timestamp, sizeof(timestamp));
 #endif
 
